      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.1 Classical Tamil Literature"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Tamil is one of the classical languages of the world and the oldest living classical language. The Tamil literary tradition spans over 2,000 years.",
      "id": "f5mjd7t"
    },
    {
      "id": "7ul7o46",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Period</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Name</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Works / Contribution</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sangam Age (300 BCE–300 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Various poets</p>",
            "<p>Purananuru, Akananuru, Pattinappalai, Tirumurugattuppatai</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Post-Sangam (200–600 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Thiruvalluvar</p>",
            "<p>Tirukkural – 1330 couplets on virtue, wealth, love</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bhakti Period (600–900 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Nayanmars (63)</p>",
            "<p>Tevaram – Shiva devotional hymns</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bhakti Period (600–900 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Alvars (12)</p>",
            "<p>Divya Prabandham – Vishnu devotional hymns</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Medieval (900–1200 CE)</p>",
            "<p>Kambar</p>",
            "<p>Ramavataram (Tamil Ramayana)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Medieval</p>",
            "<p>Sekkizhar</p>",
            "<p>Periya Puranam – lives of 63 Nayanmars</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Modern (19th–20th CE)</p>",
            "<p>Subramania Bharati</p>",
            "<p>Kannan Pattu, Kuyil Pattu; nationalist poetry</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "jdbwoax",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.2 Classical Dances"
    },
    {
      "id": "1zlksod",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Bharatanatyam"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<img alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/nrbvEE7VC0df_m1plEiKhkJnhkfzArXadvzS_pyjoz2ThMCF5Z99IH-oVCGi4XvUszD8UJD2GqfzpK-VWo97XlOsGpw9iBDCsgwxiTBIauJXoQptH2pZURuqnub3xoqifCx3VImvyWdZb8N1CgQYSxFn-o0SXZ-9ES_lj9gAIvjjrqIdDUs30iGcYqf-tynz?purpose=fullsize\" src=\"/books/Guide/TamilNadu CONSTABLE/images/image_mwfzs47.jpeg\"/>Bharatanatyam is the most celebrated classical dance of Tamil Nadu, originally performed in Hindu temples (Devadasi tradition). It was revived and brought to the stage by E. Krishna Iyer and Rukmini Devi Arundale in the early 20th century. Rukmini Devi founded Kalakshetra in Adyar, Chennai in 1936.",
      "id": "jadz0nr"
    },
    {
      "id": "odd53oq",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Based on Natyashastra and Abhinaya Darpana",
        "Combines Nritta (pure dance), Nritya (expressive dance), and Natya (drama)",
        "Karanas are 108 dance units described in Chidambaram Nataraja temple"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "r06hfwi",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Other Dance Forms"
    },
    {
      "id": "6grbvq4",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Karagattam: Pot-balancing folk dance; popular in festivals",
        "Kummi: Circular clapping dance by women; associated with festivals",
        "Kolattam: Stick dance",
        "Oyilattam: Folk dance with cloth movements",
        "Therukoothu: Street theatre combining music, dance, drama"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "405li6b",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.3 Music"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<img src=\"/books/Guide/TamilNadu CONSTABLE/images/image_k893awm.png\"/>Tamil Nadu is the heartland of Carnatic music, one of the two main traditions of Indian classical music (the other being Hindustani). The Trinity of Carnatic Music – Tyagaraja (Telugu/Tamil), Muthuswami Dikshitar, and Syama Sastri – all lived in the Thanjavur region in the 18th–19th centuries.",
      "id": "mokzja7"
    },
    {
      "id": "4zqhjha",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Tyagaraja: Composed primarily in Telugu; born in Tiruvarur",
        "Muthuswami Dikshitar: Composed in Sanskrit, Tamil, Telugu; born in Tiruvarur",
        "Syama Sastri: Composed in Telugu; born in Tiruvarur",
        "Music Academy, Chennai: Premier institution for Carnatic music; annual festival during December–January (Margazhi season)",
        "Nadaswaram: Wind instrument used in temple festivals; unique to Tamil Nadu"
      ]
    },
    {
